/* Original teaching notes. AQA references identify scope, not copied wording.
   P = Physics only; H = Higher tier only; PH = both restrictions.
   Each block has one scope so filters never hide shared material with an extension. */
const GCSE_NOTES = (() => {
  const n=(id,title,spec,scope,text,equations=[],example='',practical='')=>({id,title,spec,physicsOnly:scope.includes('P'),higher:scope.includes('H'),paragraphs:text.split('|'),equations,example,practical});
  const topics=[
    {id:'energy',title:'Energy',paper:1,sections:[
      n('stores','Following an energy transfer','4.1.1.1','',
        'Start by choosing a system: the objects you are studying. Identify which energy stores decrease and which increase. Useful store names are kinetic, thermal, chemical, gravitational, elastic, magnetic, electrostatic and nuclear. Heating, electrical work, mechanical work and radiation describe transfers, not extra stores.|For a battery-powered fan, the battery’s chemical store decreases while the moving blades gain kinetic energy and the surroundings warm up. At steady speed the blades’ kinetic energy stops increasing, but energy is still transferred to the air and surroundings. A stationary object can have energy: a raised book has a gravitational store relative to the floor.|In a closed system the total energy stays constant. Energy described as wasted has spread into stores that are less useful for the intended task; it has not disappeared. In a Sankey diagram, arrow widths represent energy amounts and the total output width equals the input width.'),
      n('mechanical-energy','Kinetic, gravitational and elastic energy','4.1.1.2','',
        'Use kilograms, metres and metres per second before substituting. The speed and extension terms are squared: doubling speed gives four times the kinetic energy, whereas doubling mass gives twice the kinetic energy. For gravitational energy, height is the vertical change, not the distance travelled along a ramp.|For a spring, extension means stretched length minus original length. The simple elastic-energy equation applies in the linear region of the force–extension relationship. When energy losses are negligible, a falling object’s decrease in gravitational energy equals its increase in kinetic energy.',
        ['Eₖ = ½mv² — energy Eₖ (J), mass m (kg), speed v (m/s)','ΔEₚ = mgΔh — g (N/kg), vertical height change Δh (m)','Eₑ = ½ke² — spring constant k (N/m), extension e (m)'],
        'A 0.80 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m/s has Eₖ = 0.5 × 0.80 × 3.0² = 3.6 J. At 6.0 m/s it has 14.4 J, not 7.2 J.'),
      n('heating','Specific heat capacity','4.1.1.3','',
        'Temperature indicates how hot something is; it is not a measure of the total energy in an object. A large bath of warm water can have a larger thermal store than a small cup of hotter water. Specific heat capacity tells you the energy needed per kilogram for each degree of temperature rise.|Use the temperature difference, not the final temperature. A temperature change has the same numerical value in kelvin and degrees Celsius. This equation describes temperature changes without a change of state; use latent heat during melting or boiling.',
        ['ΔE = mcΔθ — energy change (J), mass (kg), specific heat capacity (J/kg °C), temperature change (°C)'],
        'Heating 0.50 kg of water through 12 °C, using c = 4200 J/kg °C, requires 0.50 × 4200 × 12 = 25 200 J.',
        'Specific heat capacity: measure the mass of a block, fit a heater and thermometer, insulate it, and record temperature against supplied electrical energy. Find E from a joulemeter or E = VIt. Plot temperature rise against energy, or calculate c = E/(mΔθ). Good thermal contact and insulation reduce losses. Energy escaping to the room makes the calculated c too large. Allow for the block and thermometer taking time to reach the same temperature; hot equipment must cool before handling.'),
      n('power','Power and efficiency','4.1.1.4; 4.1.2.2','',
        'Power measures how quickly energy is transferred. Two motors can do the same work but have different powers if they take different times. A watt is a joule transferred per second. Convert minutes to seconds when calculating power in watts.|Efficiency compares the useful output with the total input over the same interval. It can be a decimal between zero and one, or a percentage between zero and one hundred. Do not compare an energy in joules directly with a power in watts.',
        ['P = E/t = W/t — power (W), energy or work (J), time (s)','efficiency = useful energy output / total energy input','efficiency = useful power output / total power input'],
        'A motor receives 600 J and lifts a load by transferring 450 J to its gravitational store. Efficiency = 450/600 = 0.75 = 75%. The remaining 150 J is transferred elsewhere.'),
      n('dissipation','Reducing unwanted transfers','4.1.2.1','',
        'Friction warms contacting surfaces. Lubrication reduces friction and therefore reduces the energy transferred by heating for the same movement. Insulation reduces the rate at which a warmer object transfers energy to cooler surroundings.|For otherwise comparable walls, greater thickness and lower thermal conductivity reduce heat transfer. Trapped air is useful because it conducts poorly and cannot circulate freely in small pockets. A building still needs heating when energy leaves it; insulation slows this loss rather than producing energy.'),
      n('efficiency-improvements','Explaining efficiency improvements','4.1.2.2','H',
        'Name the intended useful transfer before suggesting an improvement. Then identify an unwanted pathway and explain how the change reduces it. For example, reducing friction in a motor means a greater fraction of the input can lift a load rather than heat bearings.|An improvement may reduce total energy consumption for the same useful output. A more powerful device is not automatically more efficient: power concerns the rate of transfer, whereas efficiency concerns the fraction that is useful.'),
      n('insulation-practical','Comparing thermal insulation','4.1.2.1','P',
        'A fair insulation comparison changes one feature at a time. Compare materials at the same thickness, or compare thicknesses of one material. Larger temperature drops over a fixed interval indicate faster cooling, provided starting conditions are comparable.',[], '',
        'Thermal insulation: wrap identical containers in different insulators, add equal masses of water at the same starting temperature, and use identical lids. Measure temperature at regular times. Keep exposed area, room conditions and thermometer depth constant. Repeat trials and compare mean cooling curves. Avoid burns from hot water. The room temperature limits how far the water can cool, so comparing trials with very different starting temperatures is misleading.'),
      n('resources','Choosing energy resources','4.1.3','',
        'Coal, oil, natural gas and nuclear fuels are finite resources. Wind, solar, hydroelectric, tidal, wave, geothermal and sustainably replaced biofuels are renewable resources. Renewable does not mean impact-free or continuously available.|Compare options for a particular job. Transport needs portable energy; electricity supply must match demand; heating depends on the building and location. Wind and sunlight vary with weather, while tidal timing is predictable but generation is not continuous. Storage and a mixture of sources can help manage variations.|Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide; some fuels also cause air pollution. Nuclear power has low direct carbon emissions but creates radioactive waste. Dams alter habitats, turbines affect landscapes, and biofuel crops use land. Use evidence about cost, reliability, construction, pollution and local circumstances instead of declaring one resource universally best.')
    ]},
    {id:'electricity',title:'Electricity',paper:1,sections:[
      n('charge','Charge, current and potential difference','4.2.1.1–4.2.1.3','',
        'Current is the rate at which charge passes a point. A complete conducting loop and a source of potential difference are needed for a sustained current. Charges are already present throughout a metal wire; a lamp does not wait for electrons to travel all the way from the battery.|Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge. Resistance describes how much potential difference is needed to produce a given current. Charge is conserved: components transfer energy, but they do not use up current.|An ammeter goes in series with the component. A voltmeter goes across its two terminals, in parallel. Practise drawing standard symbols for cells, batteries, switches, lamps, resistors, variable resistors, diodes, LEDs, thermistors, LDRs, fuses and meters.',
        ['Q = It — charge Q (C), current I (A), time t (s)','V = IR — potential difference V (V), resistance R (Ω)','E = QV — energy E (J)'],
        'A 0.40 A current flows for 90 s: Q = 36 C. If those charges cross a 6.0 V supply, the electrical energy transferred is 36 × 6.0 = 216 J.'),
      n('components','Resistance and component characteristics','4.2.1.4','',
        'For an ohmic resistor at constant temperature, current is proportional to potential difference: its I–V graph is a straight line through the origin. On a graph with I vertically and V horizontally, a steeper straight line means lower resistance.|A filament lamp heats up as current increases. Its resistance rises, so the I–V curve becomes less steep. A diode allows a substantial current mainly in one direction. An ordinary NTC thermistor has lower resistance when warmer; an LDR has lower resistance in brighter light. These properties allow temperature and light sensing.|Calculate R = V/I at a chosen operating point for a non-ohmic component. Do not assume that resistance has one fixed value everywhere on its curve.',[], '',
        'I–V characteristics: connect an ammeter in series and a voltmeter across the test component. Use a variable resistor or adjustable low-voltage supply to obtain several pairs of readings. Reverse supply polarity for negative values. Limit current and switch off between readings to avoid overheating; keep the fixed resistor’s temperature stable. Draw a smooth curve or best-fit straight line as appropriate.'),
      n('circuits','Series, parallel and resistance investigations','4.2.2; 4.2.1.3','',
        'A series circuit has one route: the same current passes through each component, and the supply potential difference is shared. Add the individual resistances to find the total. With an unchanged supply, adding a series resistor lowers the current.|Parallel branches have the same potential difference across them. Current splits at a junction and recombines later; total supply current is the sum of branch currents. Adding a parallel branch gives charge another route, reducing the equivalent resistance below that of either branch alone.|At GCSE you should explain parallel resistance qualitatively; a reciprocal-resistance formula is not needed here. Always identify which meter measures a branch current and which measures total current.',
        ['Rtotal = R₁ + R₂ + … (series)','Itotal = I₁ + I₂ + … (at a junction)'],
        'A 4 Ω and an 8 Ω resistor in series across 6 V have total resistance 12 Ω. Current = 6/12 = 0.50 A; the potential differences are 2 V and 4 V.',
        'Resistance: vary the length of a wire while keeping its material, diameter and temperature constant. Measure V and I and calculate R at each length. Use small currents and brief readings to limit heating. A graph of R against length should be approximately straight. Also compare combinations of resistors in series and parallel using the same low-voltage supply.'),
      n('mains','Mains electricity and electrical safety','4.2.3','',
        'A direct supply maintains one polarity; an alternating supply repeatedly reverses it. UK mains has a frequency of 50 Hz and a nominal potential difference of about 230 V. A battery provides direct potential difference.|The live wire is brown; neutral is blue; earth is green and yellow. Neutral is close to earth potential. Earth is a protective connection to an exposed metal case and normally carries no current. If live touches the earthed case, a large fault current can cause protective equipment to disconnect the supply.|A fuse melts if current is excessive. Double-insulated appliances have insulating barriers and do not require an earth connection to a metal case. Touching live while connected to earth can drive dangerous current through a person. An open switch elsewhere does not guarantee that every part of a circuit is safe. School circuit investigations use low-voltage supplies, not mains.'),
      n('electrical-power','Appliance power and the National Grid','4.2.4','',
        'A heater transfers energy to thermal stores; a motor transfers energy mechanically. A larger power rating means a greater energy transfer each second when used at its rated supply. Energy use also depends on operating time.|For a given transmitted power, a larger voltage allows a smaller current. Smaller current means less heating in transmission cables. Step-up transformers increase voltage before transmission; step-down transformers reduce it for users. This explains the Grid’s efficiency without assuming that a transformer creates energy.',
        ['P = VI; P = I²R — P (W), V (V), I (A), R (Ω)','E = Pt — E (J) when P is in W and t in s'],
        'A 1.8 kW kettle operating for 150 s transfers 1800 × 150 = 270 000 J. Convert kW to W before using seconds.'),
      n('static','Static electricity and electric fields','4.2.5','P',
        'Rubbing two suitable insulators can transfer electrons between them. The one gaining electrons becomes negative; the one losing them becomes positive. Protons remain inside nuclei. Equal and opposite charge changes preserve total charge.|Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. A charged object creates an electric field, so another charge can experience force without contact. Field arrows show the force on a positive test charge: away from a positive sphere and towards a negative sphere. Closer lines indicate a stronger field.|Near a strongly charged object, the electric field can ionise air. The air becomes conducting and a spark transfers charge. An attraction alone is not always proof of opposite net charges: charge within a neutral object can redistribute.')
    ]},
    {id:'particle-model',title:'Particle model of matter',paper:1,sections:[
      n('density','Density and particle arrangements','4.3.1.1','',
        'Density compares mass with occupied volume. In a solid, particles vibrate around fixed positions; in a liquid they stay close together but move past each other; gas particles are much more widely separated and move randomly. A particle diagram is a model: the gaps, spacing and motion explain bulk properties.|Mass is conserved during an ordinary change of state. Density can change because volume changes, not because individual particles swell or become lighter. Do not assume every solid is denser than its liquid: ice is an important exception.',
        ['ρ = m/V — density ρ (kg/m³), mass m (kg), volume V (m³)','1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³; 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³'],
        'A 240 g object occupies 80 cm³. Density = 3.0 g/cm³ = 3000 kg/m³.',
        'Density: use a balance for mass. Measure several dimensions of a regular solid and calculate volume; for an irregular insoluble solid use the water displaced. For a liquid subtract the empty container mass and read volume at eye level from the meniscus. Fully submerge irregular objects without trapped bubbles, and dry the outside of containers before weighing. Repeated dimensions reduce uncertainty.'),
      n('internal-energy','Internal energy and changes of state','4.3.1.2; 4.3.2.1–4.3.2.2','',
        'Internal energy combines the kinetic and potential energies of the particles. Heating can make particles move faster, raising temperature, or change their arrangement and separation during a change of state.|Melting and boiling require energy; freezing and condensing transfer energy out. Sublimation changes a solid directly to a gas. These are physical changes: the chemical identity of the substance stays the same and the change can be reversed.|On an ideal heating graph for a pure substance at fixed pressure, temperature is constant during a change of state even though energy is still entering. The energy changes particle potential energy rather than average kinetic energy. Between plateaux, use specific heat capacity.'),
      n('latent','Specific latent heat','4.3.2.3','',
        'Specific latent heat is energy per kilogram for a particular change of state without a temperature change. Fusion refers to melting or freezing; vaporisation refers to boiling or condensing. The reverse process transfers the same magnitude of energy in the opposite direction for a given mass.|A heating calculation can have several stages. First warm the substance to its transition temperature using mcΔθ, then change state using mL, then warm the new state if necessary. Do not apply both equations to the same energy transfer.',
        ['E = mL — energy E (J), mass m (kg), specific latent heat L (J/kg)'],
        'Melting 0.20 kg of ice already at 0 °C, with L = 334 000 J/kg, needs 66 800 J. Warming ice up to 0 °C would require additional energy.'),
      n('gas-temperature','Gas pressure and temperature','4.3.3.1','',
        'A gas exerts pressure because particles collide with the container walls. Many tiny forces produce a measurable pressure. The particles move in different directions and do not all have the same speed.|Heating a gas increases the particles’ average kinetic energy. In a sealed rigid container they collide with the walls more frequently and more forcefully, increasing pressure. Keep the amount of gas and volume fixed when making this comparison: a flexible container may expand instead.'),
      n('boyle','Gas pressure and volume','4.3.3.2','P',
        'For a fixed amount of gas held at constant temperature, reducing volume increases pressure. The average particle speed is unchanged, but wall collisions happen more frequently in the smaller space. Pressure is inversely proportional to volume: halving the volume doubles pressure.|The temperature and amount of gas conditions matter. Wait for a compressed gas to return to its original temperature before comparing readings. Use the same volume units on both sides of the equation and absolute gas pressures.',
        ['pV = constant; p₁V₁ = p₂V₂'],
        'A gas occupies 60 cm³ at 100 kPa. At the same temperature, compressing it to 40 cm³ gives p₂ = 100 × 60/40 = 150 kPa.'),
      n('gas-work','Doing work on a gas','4.3.3.3','PH',
        'Compressing a gas requires a force to move a boundary such as a pump piston. The work transfers energy into the gas. If compression happens quickly enough that little energy escapes, internal energy increases and temperature rises.|This is why a bicycle pump can become warm. If the gas is then left to cool, energy is transferred to the surroundings. Distinguish this situation from the constant-temperature pressure–volume relationship: rapid compression is not automatically an isothermal process.')
    ]},
    {id:'atomic-structure',title:'Atomic structure',paper:1,sections:[
      n('atom','Inside an atom','4.4.1.1–4.4.1.2','',
        'A tiny positively charged nucleus contains protons and neutrons; electrons occupy energy levels around it. An atom has a radius of roughly 10⁻¹⁰ m and its nucleus is much smaller, of order 10⁻¹⁴ m. Most mass is concentrated in the nucleus and most of the atom is empty space.|Atomic number counts protons. Mass number counts protons plus neutrons, so neutrons = mass number − atomic number. A neutral atom has as many electrons as protons. Isotopes have the same proton number but different neutron numbers.|An electron can move to a higher energy level by absorbing energy and emit electromagnetic radiation when returning to a lower level. Removing an electron creates a positive ion; this changes the atom’s charge, not the identity of its nucleus.',[],
        'A neutral atom with atomic number 12 and mass number 25 contains 12 protons, 13 neutrons and 12 electrons. Losing two electrons leaves a 2+ ion with the same nucleus.'),
      n('models','Evidence and the nuclear model','4.4.1.3','',
        'The discovery of the electron showed that atoms contained smaller particles. The plum-pudding model placed electrons in spread-out positive charge. Alpha-scattering experiments tested that picture by directing alpha particles at thin metal foil.|Most particles passed through, suggesting largely empty space. A small fraction changed direction greatly, showing that positive charge and most mass were concentrated in a tiny region. A few rebounded, requiring a dense nucleus rather than diffuse positive charge.|Later models introduced particular electron energy levels. Evidence for protons and then neutrons refined the nucleus. Scientific models are changed when reliable observations cannot be explained by the old model; they are not simply guesses replaced by newer guesses.'),
      n('radiation','Alpha, beta, gamma and nuclear equations','4.4.2.1–4.4.2.2','',
        'An unstable nucleus can decay and emit ionising radiation. Activity is the number of decays per second, measured in becquerels. A detector records only the emissions it detects, so count rate is not necessarily the source’s total activity.|Alpha particles contain two protons and two neutrons. They ionise strongly but are stopped by paper or the outer skin layer. Beta-minus particles are fast electrons formed when a neutron changes into a proton; thin aluminium can stop them. Gamma rays are electromagnetic radiation with no charge or mass and require substantial shielding for attenuation.|Balance mass numbers and atomic numbers in nuclear equations. Alpha emission reduces them by four and two respectively. Beta-minus emission leaves mass number unchanged and increases atomic number by one. Gamma emission changes neither. Radiation penetration, range and ionisation are different properties.'),
      n('half-life','Random decay and half-life','4.4.2.3','',
        'You cannot predict when one particular nucleus will decay. A large collection has a predictable statistical trend: in each half-life the expected number of undecayed nuclei halves. The same idea applies to activity.|To read a half-life from a graph, choose an activity and find how long it takes to reach half that value. Check another pair of points. The curve flattens because fewer unstable nuclei remain, not because the surviving nuclei have become less likely to decay.|Subtract measured background count rate before interpreting the source’s decay curve. Take counts over longer intervals and repeat to reduce the relative effect of random fluctuations. An individual short reading can rise even though the long-term trend falls.'),
      n('half-life-ratios','Repeated half-lives and decay ratios','4.4.2.3','H',
        'After each half-life multiply the remaining fraction by one half. The fraction that has decayed is one minus the fraction remaining. Keep those two quantities distinct when a question asks for the decline.|If the elapsed time is an exact number of half-lives, repeated halving is often simpler than using powers. Work with background-corrected activity or count rate. Retain sensible precision because experimental measurements fluctuate.',
        ['remaining fraction = (½)ⁿ, where n = elapsed time / half-life'],
        'A corrected count rate falls from 640 to 80 counts/s in 18 h: 640 → 320 → 160 → 80 is three half-lives, so the half-life is 6 h. The remaining fraction is 1/8; the decline is 7/8.'),
      n('radiation-safety','Contamination, irradiation and evidence','4.4.2.4','',
        'Irradiation means radiation reaches an object from a source. Contamination means radioactive material is on or inside it, so exposure can continue until that material is removed or decays. Ordinary irradiation does not make an object radioactive.|Reduce exposure time, increase distance and use suitable shielding. Handling procedures also prevent contamination. Alpha emitters are especially hazardous inside the body because they deposit energy over short distances; penetration matters for sources outside it.|When evaluating a radiation study, consider dose, sample size, control groups, other risk factors and uncertainty. Peer review allows other scientists to check methods and conclusions. A claim about risk needs evidence, not just an alarming description.'),
      n('medical','Background radiation and medical applications','4.4.3','P',
        'Natural exposure includes cosmic radiation and radioactive materials in rocks; radon gas can accumulate in buildings. Medical procedures and some industrial activities add exposure. Occupation, altitude and geology can affect dose. A sievert measures biological risk from exposure; 1 Sv = 1000 mSv.|A tracer should emit radiation detectable outside the body and have a half-life long enough for the investigation but short enough to limit prolonged exposure. In radiotherapy, radiation damages unwanted tissue, with the beam arrangement chosen to limit healthy-tissue exposure.|A short half-life can mean high activity for a given number of nuclei, while a long half-life can mean material remains hazardous for a long time. Judge choices using activity, radiation type, location and intended use together. Compare the benefit of diagnosis or treatment with the exposure risk.'),
      n('nuclear','Fission, chain reactions and fusion','4.4.4','P',
        'In induced fission, a large nucleus absorbs a neutron and splits into smaller nuclei. Released neutrons can trigger further fissions, forming a chain reaction. Energy appears mainly as kinetic energy of the products and is transferred to the surroundings. Reactor control systems limit the rate of the chain reaction.|Fusion combines light nuclei into a heavier nucleus and releases energy when the product has less mass than the initial particles. Very high temperatures are needed so positively charged nuclei can approach despite electrical repulsion. Fusion powers stars.|Do not describe either process as a chemical reaction involving electrons. Both change nuclei. Fission splits; fusion joins. Diagrams should show the incoming neutron and released neutrons for fission, and two light nuclei approaching for fusion.')
    ]},
    {id:'forces',title:'Forces',paper:2,sections:[
      n('force-basics','Scalars, vectors, weight and resultant force','4.5.1','',
        'A scalar has size only, such as distance, speed, time or energy. A vector also has direction, such as displacement, velocity, acceleration or force. A journey can have a large distance but zero displacement if it ends where it began.|Contact forces include friction, tension, drag and support forces. Gravity, magnetic and electrostatic forces act without contact. Mass is measured in kilograms; weight is a gravitational force measured in newtons and can change between planets without a change in mass.|Choose a positive direction when combining forces along a line. Opposing forces subtract; forces in the same direction add. Zero resultant force does not imply that no forces act.',
        ['W = mg — weight W (N), mass m (kg), field strength g (N/kg)'],
        'A 12 N force right and a 7 N force left give 5 N right. A 60 kg person in a 9.8 N/kg field weighs 588 N.'),
      n('vectors','Free-body diagrams and vector resolution','4.5.1.4','H',
        'A free-body diagram shows the forces on one chosen object, not the forces it exerts on its surroundings. Draw arrows in the directions of the forces and label them. Balanced forces on that object have a zero vector sum.|For non-parallel forces use a stated scale. Place arrows head-to-tail; the resultant joins the starting point to the final arrowhead. A single force can be represented by two perpendicular components with the same combined effect. Measure the resultant magnitude and direction carefully rather than adding arrow lengths as scalars.'),
      n('work-springs','Work, deformation and Hooke’s law','4.5.2–4.5.3','',
        'Work is energy transferred when a force acts through a displacement along its line of action. Work against friction increases thermal energy. A newton-metre of work equals one joule.|Elastic deformation is reversed when the force is removed; inelastic deformation leaves a permanent change. A spring follows F = ke only in its proportional region. Extension is the increase in length, not its total length. A straight force–extension graph through the origin supports proportionality.',
        ['W = Fs — work (J), force (N), displacement along force (m)','F = ke — k (N/m), extension e (m)','Eₑ = ½ke² (linear spring)'],
        'A spring extends by 0.040 m under 8.0 N, so k = 8.0/0.040 = 200 N/m. Stored energy = 0.5 × 200 × 0.040² = 0.16 J.',
        'Force and extension: secure a spring beside a vertical ruler, measure its unloaded length, then add known weights in steps. Read at eye level after oscillations settle. Calculate extension and plot force against extension; its linear gradient is k. Remove loads to check whether the spring returns to its starting length. Use a stable stand and keep feet clear of falling masses.'),
      n('moments','Moments, levers and gears','4.5.4','P',
        'A moment is a turning effect about a pivot. Use the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the force’s line of action. Moving the same force farther from the pivot increases its moment. Balanced rotation requires equal total clockwise and anticlockwise moments.|A long lever lets a smaller effort produce the same turning effect as a larger force nearer the pivot. Meshing gears turn in opposite directions; a larger driven gear turns more slowly but can provide a greater turning effect. These devices transfer energy rather than create it.',
        ['M = Fd — moment M (N m), force F (N), perpendicular distance d (m)'],
        'A 15 N load acts 0.20 m from a pivot. A balancing force 0.50 m on the other side is F = (15 × 0.20)/0.50 = 6.0 N.'),
      n('pressure','Fluid and atmospheric pressure','4.5.5.1.1; 4.5.5.2','P',
        'Fluids are liquids and gases. Pressure causes a force perpendicular to a surface. For a fixed force, spreading it over a larger area lowers pressure. Convert square centimetres carefully: 1 cm² is 10⁻⁴ m².|Atmospheric pressure comes from the air above and molecular collisions with surfaces. At greater altitude there is less air above, so pressure is lower. Air density also generally decreases with altitude. Pressure acts in all directions, not just downwards.',
        ['p = F/A — pressure p (Pa), normal force F (N), area A (m²)'],
        'A 200 N force over 0.025 m² gives 8000 Pa. Doubling area with the same force halves this pressure.'),
      n('upthrust','Liquid depth, upthrust and floating','4.5.5.1.2','PH',
        'The deeper a point is below a liquid surface, the greater the weight of liquid above it. Greater liquid density also gives greater pressure at the same depth. The pressure difference across a submerged object creates an upward resultant force called upthrust.|An object floats in equilibrium when upthrust balances its weight. If its weight exceeds the available upthrust, it sinks. A hollow ship can float even though its metal is dense because its overall mass divided by displaced volume is lower.',
        ['p = hρg — pressure due to liquid (Pa), depth h (m), density ρ (kg/m³), g (N/kg)'],
        'At 2.0 m depth in water, using ρ = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 9.8 N/kg, the extra pressure due to the water is 19 600 Pa. Atmospheric pressure is additional.'),
      n('motion','Speed, acceleration and motion graphs','4.5.6.1','',
        'Average speed uses the total distance divided by total time, including pauses. Velocity also states direction. Typical walking, running and cycling speeds are around 1.5, 3 and 6 m/s, but real values vary.|The gradient of a straight distance–time graph is speed. A horizontal line means no change of distance. On a velocity–time graph, the gradient is acceleration; a horizontal line means constant velocity, not necessarily rest. Deceleration means slowing down.|Use v² − u² = 2as only for uniform acceleration. Near Earth, free fall without significant air resistance has acceleration about 9.8 m/s². A falling object in a fluid can reach terminal velocity when its resultant force becomes zero.',
        ['speed = distance/time','a = (v − u)/t — acceleration (m/s²)','v² − u² = 2as — initial velocity u, final velocity v (m/s), displacement s (m)'],
        'A cyclist increases velocity from 2 to 8 m/s in 3 s: a = (8 − 2)/3 = 2 m/s².'),
      n('motion-higher','Tangents, areas and circular motion','4.5.6.1.3–4.5.6.1.5','H',
        'To estimate instantaneous speed on a curved distance–time graph, draw a tangent at the chosen time and calculate its gradient using widely separated points on the tangent. Do not use points on the curve away from the chosen time.|The signed area under a velocity–time graph gives displacement. Split the area into rectangles and triangles or estimate it by counting squares. If velocity changes sign, total distance adds the magnitudes of the separate areas.|At constant speed around a circle, velocity changes because direction changes. There is therefore an acceleration even though the speedometer reading stays constant.',[],
        'Velocity rises uniformly from 0 to 12 m/s over 5 s. Displacement is the triangular area: ½ × 5 × 12 = 30 m.'),
      n('terminal','Explaining a terminal-velocity graph','4.5.6.1.5','P',
        'Initially a released object has little drag, so weight produces a downward resultant. As speed increases, drag increases and the acceleration decreases. The velocity–time curve becomes progressively less steep until it is horizontal at terminal velocity.|Opening a parachute suddenly increases drag. If drag exceeds weight, the downward-moving parachutist slows down. Drag then decreases as speed falls, until a lower terminal velocity is reached. Weight has not vanished at either terminal velocity; it is balanced by drag.'),
      n('newton','Newton’s laws','4.5.6.2','',
        'First law: zero resultant force means constant velocity, which includes remaining at rest. An engine driving at steady speed balances resistance; it does not need a forward resultant just to keep moving.|Second law: acceleration depends on the resultant force and mass. For fixed mass, doubling the resultant force doubles acceleration; for fixed force, doubling mass halves acceleration. Third-law force pairs are equal and opposite forces of the same interaction acting on different objects. They do not cancel on a single free-body diagram.',
        ['F = ma — resultant force F (N), mass m (kg), acceleration a (m/s²)'],
        'A 900 kg car has a 2700 N forward resultant, so a = 3.0 m/s². If its engine force is 3500 N, resistance must total 800 N.',
        'Acceleration practical: use a trolley with a pulley and hanging mass, with light gates to measure acceleration. To vary driving force at constant total moving mass, transfer masses from the trolley to the hanger. For a mass investigation keep driving force fixed. Account for friction, release without pushing, repeat readings and stop the trolley before it reaches the bench edge.'),
      n('inertia','Inertia and inertial mass','4.5.6.2.1–4.5.6.2.2','H',
        'Inertia is the tendency to continue at rest or with unchanged velocity. Inertial mass quantifies resistance to acceleration: it is the ratio of resultant force to acceleration. A larger inertial mass needs a larger force for the same acceleration.|When a bus brakes, an unrestrained passenger tends to keep moving forwards. A seat belt supplies the force needed to change the passenger’s velocity. Inertia is not an extra forward force acting on the passenger.', ['m = F/a']),
      n('stopping','Stopping distance and road safety','4.5.6.3','',
        'Stopping distance is thinking distance plus braking distance. Thinking distance is the distance travelled during reaction time. Tiredness, distraction, alcohol and drugs can increase reaction time, often beyond the roughly 0.2–0.9 s range seen in simple reaction tests.|Braking distance depends on speed, road grip, tyre and brake condition, mass and braking force. Brakes transfer kinetic energy to thermal stores. With the same braking force, doubling speed gives four times the initial kinetic energy and approximately four times the braking distance.|Use a ruler-drop test to compare reaction times, with repeated trials and a consistent release method. Large decelerations require large forces and can injure occupants. Distinguish a factor affecting reaction from a factor affecting tyre grip.',
        ['thinking distance = speed × reaction time'],
        'At 20 m/s with a reaction time of 0.70 s, thinking distance is 14 m. If braking distance is 26 m, stopping distance is 40 m.'),
      n('stopping-graphs','Comparing stopping-distance graphs','4.5.6.3.1','P',
        'A thinking-distance graph is straight through the origin if reaction time stays constant. A braking-distance graph typically curves upwards as speed increases because kinetic energy depends on speed squared. Add the two distances at each speed to obtain a stopping-distance graph.|When comparing vehicles, read values at the same speed and check whether road conditions and reaction times are comparable. Estimate between plotted values carefully; extrapolation beyond the measured range is less reliable. A larger gap between two curves need not mean the drivers have different reaction times: braking performance may differ.'),
      n('braking-forces','Estimating braking forces','4.5.6.3.4','H',
        'Choose a model and state its assumptions. If a stopping time is known, estimate average acceleration from velocity change divided by time, then use F = ma. If braking distance is known, equate work by the braking force to the reduction in kinetic energy.|These calculations estimate average resultant forces. Real forces vary during a stop and resistance may contribute. A negative acceleration sign indicates direction; the magnitude of braking force is positive.',[],
        'A 1200 kg car slows from 15 m/s to rest in 3 s. Acceleration is −5 m/s², giving an average resultant of −6000 N: 6000 N opposite the initial motion.'),
      n('momentum','Momentum and conservation','4.5.7.1–4.5.7.2','H',
        'Momentum combines mass and velocity, so direction matters. In a closed system with no external resultant force, total momentum before an interaction equals total momentum afterwards. Momentum can be transferred between objects during a collision or explosion.|Choose a positive direction before substituting into p = mv. Objects moving the opposite way have negative velocities. Kinetic energy need not be conserved in a collision even though momentum is. In an explosion from rest, the pieces have momenta in opposing directions whose vector sum is zero.',
        ['p = mv — momentum p (kg m/s)'],
        'A 2 kg trolley moving at 3 m/s has momentum 6 kg m/s in its direction of travel.'),
      n('collision-calculations','Calculating collision outcomes','4.5.7.2','PH',
        'Write total momentum before = total momentum afterwards. Choose one positive direction and keep signed velocities throughout the calculation. If the objects stick together, multiply their combined mass by their common final velocity.|For a rebound or explosion, solve for a signed velocity and interpret a negative answer as motion opposite your chosen positive direction. Check that your before and after totals agree. Momentum conservation applies when external forces on the chosen system are negligible during the event.',[],
        'A 2 kg trolley at 3 m/s hits a stationary 1 kg trolley and they stick. Initial momentum = 6 kg m/s; final speed = 6/(2 + 1) = 2 m/s.'),
      n('impact','Force and change of momentum','4.5.7.3','PH',
        'A force changes momentum. Increasing the time for the same momentum change reduces average force. Airbags, crumple zones and bending the knees on landing all extend stopping time or distance.|For a rebound, velocity changes direction. The change in momentum can be larger than when an object merely stops, so signs matter. Explain the effect using a fixed momentum change rather than saying that safety equipment removes momentum.',
        ['F = Δp/Δt = mΔv/Δt'],
        'A 0.50 kg ball changes velocity from +8 to −6 m/s in 0.10 s. Δp = 0.50 × (−6 − 8) = −7 kg m/s, so the average force is −70 N.')
    ]},
    {id:'waves',title:'Waves',paper:2,sections:[
      n('wave-properties','Wave motion and measurements','4.6.1.1–4.6.1.2','',
        'Waves transfer energy without transporting the material of the medium overall. A floating marker bobs as a ripple passes rather than travelling with the ripple across the tank. Transverse oscillations are perpendicular to travel; longitudinal oscillations are parallel, creating compressions and rarefactions.|Amplitude is maximum displacement from equilibrium, not crest-to-trough height. Wavelength is the spacing of equivalent points on successive cycles. Frequency counts cycles each second and period is the time for one cycle. A larger amplitude and a larger frequency are different changes.',
        ['v = fλ — speed v (m/s), frequency f (Hz), wavelength λ (m)','T = 1/f — period T (s)'],
        'A wave with frequency 25 Hz and wavelength 0.080 m travels at 2.0 m/s. Its period is 0.040 s.',
        'Waves practical: in a ripple tank measure several wavelengths together and divide by the number of complete wavelengths. Use the vibrator frequency, or time many oscillations, to find speed from fλ. For a vibrating string, identify nodes and antinodes; neighbouring nodes are half a wavelength apart. Compare apparatus limitations such as blurred ripples and uncertain end positions. Sound speed can be measured using two microphones a known distance apart and their signal time delay.'),
      n('sound-media','Sound crossing between materials','4.6.1.2','P',
        'When sound crosses a stationary boundary, the source still sets its frequency. If the wave speed changes in the new material, wavelength changes in the same proportion because v = fλ.|Avoid assuming that a higher wave speed automatically means a higher frequency. The medium controls speed; the source controls frequency. Use consistent units when comparing sound speeds and wavelengths.',[],
        'A 1000 Hz sound has wavelength 0.34 m at 340 m/s. In a material where it travels at 1500 m/s its wavelength is 1.5 m, but its frequency remains 1000 Hz.'),
      n('reflection','Reflection and light practical','4.6.1.3','P',
        'A wave meeting a boundary can be reflected, transmitted or absorbed. For reflection, measure angles from the normal, which is perpendicular to the surface. The reflected and incident angles are equal. A rough surface sends reflected rays in many directions because its local normals differ.|Draw rays as thin straight lines with arrows, and show the normal at the point where the ray meets the surface. A plane-mirror image is virtual: the reflected rays appear to come from behind the mirror rather than actually meeting there.',[], '',
        'Reflection and refraction: trace the outline of a transparent block, send a narrow ray through it, and mark entry, exit and ray positions. Remove the block, join the marks and measure angles from normals. Repeat for several incidence angles and materials. For reflection compare a smooth mirror and rougher surfaces. Keep the ray box away from eyes and do not touch its hot lamp.'),
      n('hearing','Sound and hearing','4.6.1.4','PH',
        'A vibrating source produces pressure variations in air. These drive vibrations in the eardrum and other structures. Mechanical parts respond effectively only over a limited range of frequencies, helping explain why hearing has limits.|A typical young person’s hearing range is about 20 Hz to 20 kHz, although individuals differ and the upper limit tends to decline with age. Pitch depends mainly on frequency; loudness relates to amplitude and the ear’s response. A solid can transmit vibrations that later produce sound in air.'),
      n('ultrasound','Ultrasound, echoes and seismic evidence','4.6.1.5','PH',
        'Ultrasound has frequencies above the normal human hearing range. At a boundary between materials, some energy is reflected. The echo delay locates the boundary, allowing internal imaging or inspection for cracks. An echo travels out and back, so divide the total distance by two.|Earthquakes generate longitudinal P-waves and transverse S-waves. P-waves travel through solids and liquids; S-waves do not pass through liquids. Patterns of arrival, refraction and missing waves reveal internal layers and provide evidence for a liquid outer core. Echo sounding uses reflected sound to map underwater surfaces.',
        ['distance to reflector = wave speed × echo time / 2'],
        'An echo returns after 0.080 s in water where sound speed is 1500 m/s. Reflector distance = 1500 × 0.080/2 = 60 m.'),
      n('spectrum','Electromagnetic spectrum and hazards','4.6.2.1; 4.6.2.3–4.6.2.4','',
        'Electromagnetic waves are transverse and can travel through a vacuum. In a vacuum they all travel at about 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. From lower to higher frequency the order is radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma. Wavelength decreases along that sequence.|Applications include broadcasting with radio, satellite links and cooking with microwaves, thermal imaging with infrared, fibre-optic communication with visible light, fluorescent lamps using ultraviolet, and medical imaging or treatment using X-rays and gamma rays.|Ultraviolet can damage skin and increase cancer risk. X-rays and gamma rays are ionising and can damage cells. Risk depends on exposure, not simply the name of the wave. Changes in electrons or nuclei can emit or absorb electromagnetic radiation; gamma radiation originates in nuclei.'),
      n('refraction','Refraction ray diagrams','4.6.2.2','',
        'Refraction diagrams show a ray changing direction at a boundary. Draw the normal and measure angles from it. Light entering glass from air usually bends towards the normal; leaving the glass it bends away. A ray along the normal is not deflected.|In a rectangular block with parallel sides, the emerging ray is parallel to the original ray but displaced sideways. Mark the internal ray as well as the incoming and outgoing rays. Do not confuse the normal with the material surface.'),
      n('em-higher','Explaining refraction and electromagnetic applications','4.6.2.2–4.6.2.4','H',
        'Refraction occurs because wave speed changes between materials. At an oblique boundary one part of a wavefront changes speed first, turning the wavefront. Frequency stays constant, while wavelength changes. Different materials absorb, transmit and reflect different wavelength ranges.|An oscillating current can produce radio waves. A receiving circuit can develop an alternating current at the same frequency as the incoming radio wave. Explain applications by linking wave behaviour to the job: transmission through the atmosphere for satellite communication, absorption by food for microwave heating, and differing absorption by tissues for X-ray imaging.|An application explanation needs more than a named use. State the relevant property and how it allows energy or information to be transferred to the intended place.'),
      n('infrared-practical','Investigating infrared surfaces','4.6.2.2','',
        'Dark matt surfaces are generally better absorbers and emitters of infrared than light shiny surfaces at the same temperature. A fair comparison separates the effect of surface finish from differences in temperature, area or distance.|Temperature readings change with time, so record comparable readings promptly or follow the whole cooling curve. The detector measures radiation arriving at it; moving it farther away or pointing it differently can change the reading.',[], '',
        'Infrared practical: compare different faces of a Leslie cube filled with hot water using an infrared detector at the same distance and angle. Keep the water temperature as similar as possible between readings. Alternatively compare absorption using identical containers with different surfaces, equal liquid amounts and equal exposure to an infrared source. Repeat and control room conditions. Take care with hot water and hot lamps.'),
      n('lenses','Convex and concave lenses','4.6.3','P',
        'A convex lens converges initially parallel rays towards its principal focus. A concave lens diverges them so that they appear to come from a focus on the incident side. Focal length is the distance from lens centre to principal focus.|For a convex-lens ray diagram, a ray parallel to the axis passes through the far focus; a ray through the lens centre continues straight. With the object outside the focal length, the image is real and inverted. Inside the focal length, the image is virtual, upright and magnified. A concave lens produces an upright, diminished virtual image.|A real image can be projected onto a screen. A virtual image is located where backwards extensions appear to meet. Magnification has no units, and object and image heights must use the same units.',
        ['magnification = image height / object height'],
        'An 18 mm image of a 6 mm object has magnification 3. A ratio below 1 means a diminished image.'),
      n('colour','Visible light, colour and filters','4.6.4','P',
        'White light contains a range of visible wavelengths. An opaque object appears a colour because it reflects some wavelengths and absorbs others. A red object under white light mainly reflects red; under blue light it can look very dark because little of that light is reflected.|A colour filter transmits selected wavelengths and absorbs others. Transparent materials transmit light with little scattering; translucent materials scatter it; opaque materials do not transmit it. The colour seen depends on the illumination, the object and any filter between it and the observer.'),
      n('black-body','Black-body radiation','4.6.5.1–4.6.5.2','P',
        'Every object emits electromagnetic radiation. A perfect black body absorbs all radiation incident on it and is the strongest possible emitter at a given temperature. A good absorber is also a good emitter.|Raising temperature increases the total emitted intensity and shifts the distribution towards shorter wavelengths. A warm radiator emits mainly infrared; a much hotter object can glow visibly. A black body is an ideal model, not simply an object painted black.'),
      n('radiation-balance','Radiation balance and Earth’s temperature','4.6.5.2','PH',
        'An object’s temperature rises when it absorbs energy faster than it emits energy, and falls when emission exceeds absorption. At a steady temperature these rates balance; radiation still travels both ways.|Earth’s temperature depends on incoming sunlight, reflection back into space, absorption by the surface and atmosphere, and infrared emission. A change in cloud cover or surface reflectivity can alter the balance. Draw separate incoming, reflected and emitted arrows so that reflection is not confused with emission. Explain temperature changes in terms of rates rather than saying that radiation simply becomes trapped forever.')
    ]},
    {id:'magnetism',title:'Magnetism and electromagnetism',paper:2,sections:[
      n('magnets','Magnets and field patterns','4.7.1','',
        'Like magnetic poles repel and unlike poles attract. A permanent magnet retains its field; an induced magnet becomes magnetised in an applied field and usually loses much of that magnetism when it is removed. Attraction alone does not prove an object is a permanent magnet.|Magnetic field lines outside a bar magnet point from north to south. A compass’s north-seeking end shows the local field direction. Closely spaced lines indicate stronger field, usually near the poles. Lines do not cross because the field has one direction at each point.|To map a field, place a plotting compass at successive positions, mark its direction and join a series of marks with arrows. Earth has a magnetic field, explaining compass behaviour. Iron, steel, cobalt and nickel are examples of magnetic materials.'),
      n('electromagnets','Fields from currents and solenoids','4.7.2.1','',
        'A current produces a magnetic field around a straight wire. Field lines form concentric circles; reversing the current reverses their direction. A compass near the wire demonstrates the effect. Increasing current strengthens the field; increasing distance weakens it.|A solenoid is a coil whose individual turns combine to produce a stronger field. Inside a long solenoid the field is approximately uniform, with parallel equally spaced lines. Outside, its pattern resembles a bar magnet. An iron core increases the field strength, and switching current off makes an electromagnet release its load.'),
      n('devices','Interpreting electromagnetic devices','4.7.2.1','P',
        'Trace the sequence in a device diagram: current flows, a coil magnetises a core, an iron part experiences attraction, and something moves. In a relay, a low-current control circuit moves contacts that switch another circuit.|An electromagnetic lifting device can pick up magnetic scrap while powered and release it when switched off. Explain why iron is useful for a temporary core and why an ordinary plastic or aluminium object would not be lifted in the same way. Always relate the explanation to the parts shown in the question.'),
      n('motor-effect','Motor effect and Fleming’s left-hand rule','4.7.2.2','H',
        'A current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field can experience a force. The force is perpendicular to both the field and the current. With the left hand at right angles: first finger indicates field, second finger conventional current, and thumb force.|Reversing either current or field reverses the force; reversing both leaves its direction unchanged. Force increases with current, magnetic flux density and conductor length within the field. The simple equation here assumes the conductor is perpendicular to the field.',
        ['F = BIl — force (N), magnetic flux density B (T), current I (A), active length l (m)'],
        'A 0.12 m wire carries 3.0 A perpendicular to a 0.25 T field. Force = 0.25 × 3.0 × 0.12 = 0.090 N.'),
      n('motors','How a motor rotates','4.7.2.3','H',
        'Opposite sides of a current-carrying coil have currents in opposite directions. In a magnetic field they experience opposite forces, producing a turning effect. Electrical energy is transferred mechanically.|In a simple direct-current motor, a split-ring commutator reverses current in the coil every half-turn, keeping the turning effect in the same rotational direction. Stronger fields and larger currents increase the turning effect. Brushes maintain electrical contact with the rotating commutator.'),
      n('speakers','Loudspeakers and headphones','4.7.2.4','PH',
        'A moving-coil loudspeaker places a coil in a permanent magnet’s field. An alternating current produces a force that repeatedly changes direction. The attached cone vibrates and creates pressure variations in air.|A higher-frequency signal makes the cone oscillate faster; a larger signal amplitude produces larger motion within the device’s limits. This is the motor effect: an electrical signal causes movement. A microphone works in the reverse information-transfer direction using induction.'),
      n('induction','Induction and the generator effect','4.7.3.1','PH',
        'Moving a conductor through a magnetic field, or changing the field through a coil, can induce potential difference. Current flows only if there is a complete circuit. A stationary magnet inside a stationary coil does not provide a changing field and therefore does not sustain an induced current.|Faster relative movement, a stronger field or more coil turns generally increase induced potential difference. Reversing the movement or the magnetic field reverses polarity. The induced current produces a field opposing the change that caused it; pulling a magnet through a coil therefore requires work.'),
      n('generators','Alternators, dynamos and microphones','4.7.3.2–4.7.3.3','PH',
        'Rotating a coil in a magnetic field repeatedly changes the field through it. An alternator uses slip rings to deliver alternating potential difference. A simple dynamo uses a split-ring arrangement to produce a one-direction, pulsating output. Mechanical work supplies the electrical energy.|Faster rotation increases the frequency of the output and can increase its peak potential difference. Interpret a voltage–time graph by distinguishing peak value, period and polarity.|In a moving-coil microphone, pressure variations move a diaphragm and coil relative to a magnet. The induced potential difference follows those vibrations, converting sound information into an electrical signal. Unlike a loudspeaker, the motion is the input rather than the output.'),
      n('transformers','Transformers and power transmission','4.7.3.4','PH',
        'A transformer has two coils linked by an iron core. Alternating current in the primary produces a changing magnetic field; that changing field induces potential difference in the secondary. A steady direct current does not continuously induce a secondary voltage.|More secondary turns give a step-up transformer; fewer give a step-down transformer. For an ideal transformer, input and output powers are equal, so a voltage increase is accompanied by a current decrease. Real transformers dissipate some energy.|Use matching primary and secondary quantities in the ratios. In Grid cables the smaller current reduces I²R heating, which is why high-voltage transmission is useful.',
        ['Vₚ/Vₛ = Nₚ/Nₛ — voltage ratio equals turns ratio','VₚIₚ = VₛIₛ (100% efficient transformer)'],
        'A transformer with 600 primary turns and 150 secondary turns has 24 V applied. Vₛ = 24 × 150/600 = 6 V. For ideal operation, a 2 A secondary current needs a 0.50 A primary current.')
    ]},
    {id:'space',title:'Space physics',paper:2,sections:[
      n('solar-system','The Solar System and orbits','4.8.1.1; 4.8.1.3','P',
        'Our Sun is one star in the Milky Way galaxy. Planets orbit the Sun; moons are natural satellites of planets; artificial satellites are placed in orbit by people. The Solar System also contains dwarf planets, asteroids and comets.|Gravity supplies the force that keeps an orbiting object’s path curved. A satellite is continually falling under gravity while moving sideways fast enough to remain in orbit. It is not beyond gravity. Distinguish an orbit around a planet from that planet’s orbit around the Sun.'),
      n('stars','From nebula to stellar remnant','4.8.1.2','P',
        'Gravity pulls gas and dust in a nebula together, producing a protostar. As the material contracts its temperature rises. When conditions allow sustained fusion, the star enters its main-sequence stage. Energy released by fusion supports pressure that balances gravitational compression.|A star of roughly the Sun’s mass eventually expands into a red giant, loses its outer layers and leaves a white dwarf that cools over time. A much more massive star becomes a red supergiant and can explode as a supernova, leaving a neutron star or black hole depending on its remaining mass.|Fusion builds heavier nuclei from lighter ones. Massive stars can build elements up to iron; supernova processes produce and disperse heavier elements. Material scattered into space becomes part of later stars and planets. The original mass is central to choosing the correct life-cycle path.'),
      n('orbits-higher','Changing velocity in a circular orbit','4.8.1.3','PH',
        'In a circular orbit at steady speed, velocity changes continuously because direction changes. Gravity provides the inward resultant force and therefore the acceleration. No continuing forward thrust is needed in an ideal orbit without resistance.|For a stable circular orbit around the same central body, orbital speed and radius are linked. A different stable orbital speed requires a different radius; closer circular orbits have higher speeds. A brief thrust does not simply create a faster circular orbit at the old radius.'),
      n('redshift','Red-shift and the expanding Universe','4.8.2','P',
        'Known patterns of spectral lines from distant galaxies are usually shifted towards longer wavelengths. This red-shift indicates expansion; more distant galaxies generally show larger shifts and greater recession speeds. It is the shift of a line pattern that matters, not whether a galaxy looks visibly red.|Working backwards from expansion suggests an earlier hot, dense Universe: the Big Bang model. This describes the expansion of space, not an explosion from one point into pre-existing empty space. Observations and models are compared as new evidence becomes available.|Measurements indicate that expansion is accelerating. Dark energy is a name used for the unknown cause in the model; dark matter describes unseen matter inferred from gravitational effects. Their nature remains uncertain. Separate what is measured from the explanations proposed to account for it.')
    ]}
  ];
  const slugs=['energy','electricity','particle-model-of-matter','atomic-structure','forces','waves','magnetism-and-electromagnetism','space-physics-physics-only'];
  topics.forEach((t,i)=>{t.source='https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/physics/gcse/physics-8463/specification/subject-content/'+slugs[i];});
  return {topics,combinedSource:'https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/science/gcse/science-8464/specification/physics-subject-content',checked:'22 September 2026'};
})();
if(typeof module!=='undefined')module.exports=GCSE_NOTES;
